 ## Overview
-Clipping Geometry Illumination is a shading technique used during volume rendering to provide realistic lighting at the boundaries of user-defined clipping geometry [S1, unofficial]. While standard volume rendering relies on the volume's internal gradient for surface normals, this method substitutes the gradient with the geometric normal of the clipping boundary at the intersection point [S1, unofficial]. This distinguishes it from sibling methods like [Clipping plane for volume suppression](887-clipping-plane-for-volume-suppression.md) which may suppress data entirely, or [Pre-integrated Clipping Boundary Handling](7867-pre-integrated-clipping-boundary-handling.md) which focuses on integration artifacts; Clipping Geometry Illumination specifically addresses the visual discontinuity of lighting at the cut plane [S1, unofficial].
+Clipping Geometry Illumination is a shading technique for volume rendering that replaces the volume's internal gradient with the geometric surface normal at the boundary of user-defined clipping geometry to provide correct lighting [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of visual discontinuities and unlit surfaces that occur when parts of the volume are made transparent via clipping [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Cropping](1648-cropping.md) and differs from sibling methods such as [Variance Clipping](1970-variance-clipping.md), [Clipped Cropping Region Planes](2931-clipped-cropping-region-planes.md), [Clipping Cube and Cutting Plane Interaction](7062-clipping-cube-and-cutting-plane-interaction.md), [Depth-based Clipping for Focus and Context Volume Rendering](7485-depth-based-clipping-for-focus-and-context-volume-rendering.md), [Pre-integrated Clipping Boundary Handling](7867-pre-integrated-clipping-boundary-handling.md), [Camera and Clipping Setup](14782-camera-and-clipping-setup.md), and [Plane-based Clipping](22849-plane-based-clipping.md) by specifically substituting the gradient with the clipping geometry's surface normal for illumination [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:**
-- Volume dataset $V$ with scalar values.
-- Transfer function $T$ mapping scalar values to color and opacity.
-- Clipping geometry defined by a set of planes or surfaces.
-- Light source position and direction.
-- Camera position and view direction.
